Subject: Size a field from the class template a `replaces=` stood in for it

`<div rustbindgen replaces="X"></div>` binds `X` as some other type the header
declares, and that other type is usually a different size: the annotation
exists to substitute a Rust-shaped stand-in for a C++ type Rust cannot express.
bindgen keeps a struct holding such a field honest by measuring the field as
the type it is *written* as - resolved through the `ResolvedTypeRef` the
replacement is recorded as - and padding the struct out to the size clang
reported for it.

A class template is replaced the same way. A member which is an instantiation
of one is not covered.

    template <typename T> struct Real { T* a; T* b; T* c; };
    /** <div rustbindgen replaces="Real"></div> */
    template <typename T> struct Stand { T* p; };
    struct A { Real<int> m; };
    struct B { Real<int>* m; };

The annotation names `Real`, so the `ResolvedTypeRef` is recorded on `Real`.
`Real<int>` is an item of its own, carrying the layout clang measured for the
three pointers, and resolving *it* through type refs finds nothing to follow:
it is an instantiation, not a reference. So the field is written as
`Stand<int>`, eight bytes, while the tracker was handed twenty-four. It
concluded the field filled the struct and emitted no padding, and `A` is a
third of the width of the object C++ hands it. Every `A` crossing the boundary
is truncated, and a caller who asks C++ to construct one in storage Rust sized
gets it built past the end. `B` is a pointer either way and was always right.

What the field is written as is the instantiation's *definition*, resolved
through type refs, instantiated with the same arguments. So resolve that, and
where it lands on another item take the layout of what it landed on. The
annotation is not what keys this: a definition reached through a type ref
resolved for any other reason - a template forward-declared where it is used,
say - is equally the one codegen writes, and equally the layout to take.

Nothing instantiates a substitute, though - the header declares it to be given
to Rust in place of the name its annotation points at - so clang usually never
laid it out and it has no layout of its own. Every instantiation bindgen writes
for it does have the same one, since a member sized by a template parameter has
no layout to read either. So lay its members out the way `repr(C)` will, guarded
on the members being the whole of what the struct holds: a base class, a vtable
pointer, a bitfield allocation unit and the address byte an empty class is given
are each something else in it; a packed class is not laid out this way at all; a
flexible array member becomes a type parameter of its own under
`--flexarray-dst`; a member aligned more strictly than bindgen promises can be
preceded by padding written as a blob of that promised alignment, wider than the
gap it was asked to fill, and every such member is declined rather than only the
ones so preceded; and a vector member is written as the array of its elements,
whose alignment is the element's rather than the vector's. A union is declined
before the layout question is asked at all, because `CompInfo::layout` answers it
for one by taking the widest member without rounding up to the alignment, which
is not the size Rust gives it.

An instantiation whose definition resolves to itself keeps the layout clang
measured, and so do the two this reaches but codegen writes as a blob of that
layout anyway: one bindgen was told to keep opaque, and one whose substitute has
no parameters to take the arguments.

Every one of those declines leaves the field measured as the type C++ declared,
which is what it was measured as before any of this. Where the substitute is the
narrower of the two - which is what a substitute usually is, and why the
annotation exists - that leaves the enclosing struct short of the object C++
builds in it, exactly as short as it already was. The declines say this
arithmetic has no answer, not that the answer does not matter; what those shapes
need is a field *written* as a blob of the layout clang measured, which is a
change to what codegen emits rather than to how it is measured.

This is the walk an array field already goes through, so the two compose in
both directions: an array of instantiations of a replaced template is `N` of
the substitute, and a substitute holding an array of one is measured as such.
The walk now carries the items it is in the middle of measuring rather than the
ones it has finished with, dropping each on the way back out, because a
struct's members are siblings where an array's elements were a chain.
